   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Cochlear implants can restore hearing in people who have a nonfunctional cochlea. The implant consists of a microphone that picks up sound. A speech processor selects sounds in the range of human speech, and a transmitter converts these sounds to electrical impulses, which are then sent to the auditory nerve. \n Which of the following types of hearing loss would not be restored by a cochlear implant?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1050991,
     "content_html": "Hearing loss resulting from absence or loss of hair cells in the organ of Corti."
    },
    {
     "id": 1050992,
     "content_html": "Hearing loss resulting from an abnormal auditory nerve."
    },
    {
     "id": 1050993,
     "content_html": "Hearing loss resulting from fracture of the cochlea."
    },
    {
     "id": 1050994,
     "content_html": "Hearing loss resulting from damage to bones of the middle ear."
    }
   ],
